<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Vector's window theme.

  ## What this used to be

  One style, parented on `android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar`, with a hard-coded
  `#0c0e14` status bar, navigation bar and window background. That is the dark
  chrome palette's ground colour, applied unconditionally — so a phone in light
  mode, whose map and chrome both resolve light, still launched into a black
  window and painted black system bars behind a light app. The palette was
  designed to follow the theme; the window was not.

  ## Light first, and why the parent changed

  The brief makes the light theme the primary design target, so the default
  parent is now the LIGHT Material theme and `values-night` carries the dark one.
  The bars are transparent because the app draws edge to edge
  (`WindowCompat.setDecorFitsSystemWindows(window, false)`) and composites its own
  scrim over the map; a coloured bar would put a hard edge across the top of the
  cartography and cost the driver a strip of it.

  `windowBackground` is the design system's field colour rather than a platform
  default, so the first frame the user sees is already Vector's — this is the
  "no flash of unstyled UI" requirement, and it is why the colour lives in
  `values/colors.xml` and `values-night/colors.xml` next to each other.
-->
<resources>

    <style name="Theme.Vector" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/vector_field</item>
        <item name="android:colorBackground">@color/vector_field</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <!-- The window's own guess at bar polarity. MainActivity overrides this
             per phase and per theme through `applySystemBarAppearance`, because
             the maneuver band is dark in BOTH themes and the rule is about what
             is under the icons, not about the theme. -->
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
        <!-- Vector draws its own type; the platform must not add a fallback
             face to a window that is already composed. -->
    </style>

    <!--
      Launch.

      A dedicated splash theme so the pre-Compose frames are Vector's mark on
      Vector's field rather than a white or black flash. `postSplashScreenTheme`
      hands over to `Theme.Vector`, and `MainActivity.installSplashScreen()`
      keeps the splash up only until the first composition, so a cold start shows
      the mark and then the app — never a bare window.

      The icon is `ic_splash_mark`, which is original artwork drawn for this
      identity. The platform masks it, so it keeps its ink inside the central
      two-thirds.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Vector.Splash" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/vector_field</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_splash_mark</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.Vector</item>
    </style>
</resources>
